  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nAfter a brief bout of shortwave ridging departs on Friday, deep \nlayer southwest flow strengthens on Saturday as a mid-level trough \napproaches. Deep layer moisture increase is noted in both the HREF \nand REFS with mean PWAT values near 2\" which is above the 99th \npercentile climatology for the day setting the stage for heavy rain \npotential. MRMS soil moisture remains elevated across the region at \n>50%. Increasing low-level warm advection and broad ascent \ndownstream of the trough should support a shield of precipitation \narriving from west to east in the late morning hours. Ahead of that \nshield, CAMs fire off additional convection as convective \ntemperatures are reached. This could set the stage for a couple \nrounds of heavy rain especially for the PA and WV ridges with the \ninitial convection and then the shield of precip shortly behind it. \nHREF and REFS probability for >1\" of rain through Saturday night \nboth exceed 80% east of Pittsburgh with a long-tailed distribution \nextending farther west which suggests some localized convective \nenhancement and higher amounts can't be ruled out farther west \neither. Again east of Pittsburgh, probability of >2\" is as high as \n70% and >3\" as high as 40%, with HREF LPMM values nearing 6\" and a \nrepresentation of a localized high-end scenario. For these reasons, \nbecoming increasingly concerned about flash flooding in the higher \nelevation, and have issued a Flood Watch for far southeastern OH, \nnorthern WV, and southwestern PA where the ensemble probabilities \nare highest.\n\nPerhaps with storm-scale processes as we lack a defined surface \nforcing mechanism, CAMs suggest convective enhancement and even\nsome linear/bowing structure along with the initial push of \nshowers and storms in the late morning as it moves through \neastern OH and into western PA. Despite the deep layer moisture,\nHREF joint probability for >1000 J/kg of SBCAPE and 30 knots of\ndeep layer shear is up to 80% across a bulk of eastern OH and \nwestern PA. While the thermal profile shows more deeper, skinny \nCAPE, can't be blind to a severe threat, primarily wind, along \nwith the flooding. Should see the precip make its exit stage \nright by midnight and just leave some scattered showers around \novernight.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nNo rest for the weary as we face another severe threat again on \nSunday afternoon as a deeper mid-level trough approaches and slides \njust off to our north. This will kick a surface cold front through \nthe Great Lakes and to our local area in the afternoon hours. Plenty \nof surface moisture will be in place from Saturday's rain, and light \nsoutherly flow will reinforce the higher dew points as ensemble \nmeans maintain upper 60s Saturday night through Sunday morning. \nPrimary uncertainty headed into the afternoon is how much clearing \nwe can get to destabilize. NBM 25th/75th spread in cloud cover at \n11am ranges from partly cloudy to overcast, so the goal posts are \nstill wide. Consequently, NBM's mean SBCAPE come 5pm sits around 900 \nJ/kg, but the 25th percentile is as low as 300-500 J/kg and the 75th \npercentile nearing 2000 J/kg. It's likely not going to be a day \nwhere we need to violently destabilize as deep layer shear will be \nstrong (35-45 knots) beneath an increasing belt of 40-60 knot \nwesterlies at 500 mb, and stronger forcing along the front should be \nenough to overcome lower CAPE values. Interestingly, some members \nwithin the LREF thermal profiles, as well as some deterministic \nguidance, shows a subsident warm layer around 800 mb that pretty \neffectively caps updrafts. This is likely a result of a stronger \nsurface ridge extending in locally from departing high pressure\noff the New England coast. The latest RRFS does not have this \nfeature. So that will be another aspect to add to the \nuncertainty in place still, and will see how future CAMs come \nin.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\nAlbeit with some timing differences, the Sunday trough departs \nsometime on Monday and will return a brief bout of dry weather \nto start off the work week as mid-level ridging builds. Can't \nrule out a few showers continuing into Monday morning in a \nsolution with a slower, deeper departing trough, but in any \nscenario, we should be dry by Monday afternoon.",
